            'content' => '<p>Years ago, I joined a prayer meeting and heard members of the prayer group claim various people they knew to be witches, warlocks, and adulterers. Some individuals accused of wrongdoing were not part of the community and had only been mentioned by another member of the prayer group. The accusers claimed that "God revealed their sin" to them.</p>
<p>For example, a neighbor might be accused of being a witch because she gave a stern look. Could this have resulted from them receiving an accusatory glare? Of course!</p>
<p>It was later revealed that the members of the prayer group had never met the individuals they accused. Additionally, none of them sought scriptural or physical evidence to support their claims. As a result, the session appeared to be more about gossiping rather than focusing on prayer.  </p>
<p>Following these events, I created a "testing of spirits" list for those interested in understanding scriptural foundations for how to judge individuals. This list references content from the Bible and serves as a tool to offer an unbiased, scripture-based assessment of people who claim that God revealed something to them, ensuring it aligns with scripture. It is a simple tool, but certainly very effective.</p>
<p>Lastly, the following verse from Deuteronomy 11:29 compares two mountains, Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal. Mount Gerizim had the blessing, and Mount Ebal had the curses. This blessing and cursing of the mountains demonstrates that an individual can only live on one mountain at a time. Because if your foot is on one mountain, then that is where you dwell. But if your foot is on Mount Gerizim, then that is where you dwell. You cannot dwell on both mountains at the same time!</p>
<p><img src="https://localhost/app/uploads/2025/05/Flickr-ian-w-scott_8757773311_546699e7e1_o-Golan-Heights-Mount-Hermon-CC-BY-SA-Ian-Scott-300x225.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="225"></p>
<h2 style="text-align: center;">Mount Gerizim vs Mount Ěyḇal</h2>
<p>And it shall be, when יהוה your Elohim has brought you into the land which you go to possess, that you shall put the <strong>blessing on Mount Gerizim and the curse on Mount Ěyḇal</strong>. (Deut 11:29 ISR)</p>
<h2 style="text-align: center;">The Mountain Of Blessing: The Mountain Of Curse</h2>
<p><strong>☐  </strong>Deuteronomy 11:29 describes two mountains: Mount Gerizim and Mount Ěyḇal. A blessing was placed upon Mount Gerizim and a curse placed upon Mount Ěyḇal.  Why is one mountain blessed and one cursed? Because one cannot stand with a foot on each mountain at the same time! Either one is blessed or one is cursed. Discernment is always based on the Word of Yehovah!</p>
